{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q009_heme_bundle_fold_not_homology","answer":"No. SCOPe 2.08 does not treat these three groups as one homologous superfamily. It places them in one fold, f.21 "Heme-binding four-helical bundle" (sunid 81344), but in three separate superfamilies: f.21.1 "Transmembrane di-heme cytochromes" (sunid 81342), f.21.2 "Fumarate reductase respiratory complex transmembrane subunits" (sunid 81343), and f.21.3 "Respiratory nitrate reductase 1 gamma chain" (sunid 103501). The shared element is the fold-level architecture: four transmembrane helices in an up-and-down bundle that binds one or two hemes between the helices. Fold membership is an architectural grouping and does not establish common ancestry, so the prompt's premise of one homologous superfamily is not supported.

Entity by entity: the transmembrane di-heme cytochromes are superfamily f.21.1. The fumarate reductase respiratory-complex transmembrane subunits are superfamily f.21.2, which has two families: f.21.2.1 (FrdC cytochrome b subunit) and f.21.2.2 (SdhC/FrdC and SdhD/FrdD transmembrane subunits, including SDH subunits C and D). The respiratory nitrate reductase gamma chain is its own superfamily, f.21.3. SCOPe's f.21.3 note calls its subunit a "possible link" to the other two superfamilies: it corresponds to the gamma subunit of formate dehydrogenase N but is structurally closer to FrdC. That is a curator's structural observation and a stated hypothesis, not a homology assertion, and it did not lead SCOPe to merge the superfamilies. The f.21.1 note says three of four heme ligands are conserved between two families and both hemes bind similarly but not identically. I did not enumerate the f.21.1 child families, so I cannot say which two families that note refers to.

Unsafe transfers to avoid: (1) do not infer homology or a common superfamily from the shared fold, the shared heme-binding architecture, or the f.21.3 structural-similarity remark; (2) do not transfer f.21.2 family membership to f.21.1 or f.21.3 members; (3) do not treat the fold-level comment as evidence about any individual protein's function or heme-site homology. Merging these superfamilies would need explicit curated SCOPe revision or independent sequence/structure-based evolutionary evidence for shared ancestry, and this session provided neither. I used no AlphaFold, sequence-hit, or structure-hit evidence, and I did not check domain-level (SID) representatives within these superfamilies. The conclusion is limited to SCOPe 2.08 node-level classification.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81342","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81342/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81343","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81343/children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81373/children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103501","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103501/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/f.21.2/parents"]}]}
